 | Flag | Default | Description |
@@ -350,6 +364,35 @@ Caddy automatically handles WebSocket upgrades and TLS certificates.
 
 </details>
 
+## Keyhive Contact Card
+
+With `--auth keyhive` (the default) the server publishes its keyhive contact
+card as JSON on the sync port:
+
+```bash
+curl https://sync.example.com/.well-known/keyhive/contact-card.json
+```
+
+The same card is at `/contact-card.json` and `/contact-card`. A client hands
+it to keyhive to delegate access to the server. With
+`--auth open` the path answers 404.
+
+## Static Files
+
+`--static-dir <DIR>` serves the files in a directory to plain HTTP `GET`
+requests on the sync port, with `/` as `index.html`. WebSocket upgrades and
+long-poll requests are unaffected, so a server can carry a homepage at the
+same URL clients sync with.
+
+## Iroh Relay
+
+`--serve-iroh-relay` makes the server an [iroh relay](https://docs.rs/iroh-relay)
+on the sync port as well: WebSocket upgrades to `/relay` go to an in-process
+relay server, and `GET /ping` and `/generate_204` answer iroh's probes. Iroh
+endpoints use it by setting the server's URL (e.g. `https://sync.example.com`)
+as their relay URL. Anyone may relay through it. QUIC address discovery is not
+served, since it needs its own UDP port and TLS.
+
